WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:07.710
The hunt for Earth 2.0 is still on.

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:11.760
We've examined intense red dwarf systems.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.060
Massive super-Earths.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.120
And alien exomoons.

00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:23.470
But so far, there's no place like home.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:28.530
There are all these criteria we have to tick
off a sunlike star.

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:31.120
An orbit that puts it at about the right
temperature.

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:35.380
A solid surface, something that could retain
an atmosphere.

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:43.940
But a planet that appears Earth like on the
outside may not be Earth like on the inside.

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:51.670
One of the things that makes our world so
unique is its plate tectonics, and that

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:53.320
actually regulates our climate.

00:00:56.430 --> 00:01:02.250
The Earth's climate depends on cycles of
materials like carbon dioxide and water.

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:09.390
Molecules move between the Earth's molten
interior and the surface through active plate

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:11.970
tectonics and volcanic eruptions.

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:18.610
These cycles help to regulate the
temperature and composition of the Earth's

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:19.480
atmosphere.

00:01:22.690 --> 00:01:28.150
If we were to find another Earth like planet
out there and it had geologic activity, that

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:32.260
means that at least it has the means to
sustain the carbon cycle.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:37.450
And all of these natural phenomenon that
makes this planet habitable and and

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:38.380
sustainable.

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:45.290
How can we know what's happening inside a
planet?

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:52.610
A clue can be found in vast ranges across
our world.

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:54.740
Mountains.

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:08.240
These topographical features are an indicator
that the planet is alive, and there is still

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.460
processes happening underneath its surface.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:19.890
Mountain ranges are created when a planet's
tectonic plates collide.

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:28.260
And even though exoplanets are light years
away, astronomers could work out whether

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:31.260
their surfaces are smooth or covered in
peaks.

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:41.330
Those mountain ranges are poking out and
depending on which rotation the planet is in,

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:46.160
the planet will appear very slightly bigger
or very slightly smaller, depending on the

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:48.050
silhouette which is being cast.

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:58.800
These tiny changes in light could be the sign
that an exoplanet is healthy and active.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:06.600
But we can only use this method when a
planet is in front of its star.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.120
What if astronomers could use starlight
itself to determine the geology of a planet?

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:21.210
We think that planets form at roughly the
same sort of time that stars form, and they

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:23.670
all form from this same giant cloud of
material.

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:31.860
And so if you measure the composition of a
star, then it seems reasonable to take those

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:35.490
values and assume they're somewhat similar
for the planets as well.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:44.320
Astronomers can work out what chemical
elements are present in the star by splitting

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:46.810
its light into different wavelengths.

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:52.990
At any planets around that star, we'll have
a similar chemical composition.

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:58.930
Composition is actually a really important
part of whether or not it's actually going to

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:02.200
be habitable. The composition really is it's
geology.

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:13.120
Rocky exoplanets are all made from the same
basic ingredients chemical elements like

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:15.880
oxygen, silicon, and aluminum.

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:21.700
Change the balance of ingredients and you
get very different planets.

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:29.040
If we have some idea of the composition of a
rocky planet, we can actually use that to

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:34.110
give us clues as to whether a world has or
doesn't have plate tectonics.

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:43.290
New research indicates that exoplanets with
too much silicon and sodium form different

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:51.600
types of rock than those on Earth, creating
rigid planets where plate tectonics stall and

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:55.350
carbon dioxide builds up with devastating
consequences.

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:02.800
Without active geology, we end up with maybe
a Venusian atmosphere.

00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:05.140
That means there's a runaway greenhouse
effect.

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:07.600
It's gotten hotter and hotter.

00:05:07.630 --> 00:05:09.370
Gases are baked out of the rocks.

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:11.110
There's no way to actually rain them back
out.

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:13.330
Not a good place for life at all.

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:20.690
At worse, the planet becomes a pressure
cooker.

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:23.500
Waiting to explode.

00:05:24.910 --> 00:05:28.300
If we change the composition of a planet, it
affects its tectonic system.

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:30.520
That entirely changes how a planet loses
heat.

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:33.010
And the heat builds up and builds up and
builds up.

00:05:33.010 --> 00:05:36.580
And then maybe there's a catastrophic
overturn of the crust.

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:49.880
The solid crust of the planet collapses.

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:59.660
Oceans of lava bubble up and the greenhouse
atmosphere boils the surface.

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:05.480
A violent end to a potential new home.

00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:12.440
Clearly, you need to know about the
composition of those planets before you can

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:16.220
start making statements about how habitable
those worlds truly are.

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:25.660
But there's something else that a planet
needs to be Earth, like an invisible shield

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:28.630
that protects it from the dangers of space.

00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:33.340
Providing warmth and life giving water.

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:35.340
An atmosphere.

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:42.720
The hunt for Earth 2.0 has turned up plenty
of planets.

00:06:44.230 --> 00:06:49.690
But for a planet to be like Earth, it has to
check a lot of boxes.

00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:56.050
If you're really looking for Earth 2.0, then
you're going to have to find a planet that's

00:06:56.050 --> 00:07:03.160
the same mass and size as Earth, orbiting a
sunlike star at about the same distance, with

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:08.380
a similar atmosphere and a lot of surface
water that's in liquid form.

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:09.910
Good luck.

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:16.070
And on the list of requirements.

00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:19.820
An exoplanet's atmosphere is critical.

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:23.760
It protects the planet from huge temperature
swings.

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:26.400
It protects the planet from small asteroid
impacts.

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:31.090
It protects the planet from dangerous
radiation, from space and from the star.

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:35.850
It is almost literally a shield around the
planet protecting us from outer space.

00:07:37.490 --> 00:07:40.100
But it also has to be the right kind of
atmosphere.

00:07:45.730 --> 00:07:51.370
Get it wrong and the planet can have
crushing, boiling conditions on the surface.

00:07:54.040 --> 00:07:55.810
Look at our own solar system.

00:07:55.810 --> 00:08:00.660
The sun's habitable zone includes three
different planets Venus, Earth, and Mars.

00:08:00.670 --> 00:08:02.620
But Mars has a thin atmosphere and is too
cold.

00:08:03.190 --> 00:08:06.040
Venus has too thick of an atmosphere and is
too hot.

00:08:06.070 --> 00:08:09.190
We're the only planet that happens to be
just right.

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:20.540
So far, astronomers have mostly had to guess
if these exoplanets have atmospheres.

00:08:21.880 --> 00:08:25.030
But now we're looking for them directly.

00:08:26.570 --> 00:08:30.860
Searching for Earth like atmospheres around
Earth like planets.

00:08:33.430 --> 00:08:36.280
This is incredibly hard to do.

00:08:36.280 --> 00:08:41.440
So in order to to look at the details of
these atmospheres and the glare of the star

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:47.440
requires incredibly precise technology and
precise measurements.

00:08:50.020 --> 00:08:55.750
Astronomers detect atmospheres by watching a
planet pass in front of the star.

00:08:58.210 --> 00:09:02.710
A small fraction of light shines around the
edge of the planet and through the

00:09:02.710 --> 00:09:10.030
atmosphere, where molecules like water,
hydrogen, and carbon dioxide absorb

00:09:10.030 --> 00:09:12.580
particular wavelengths of light from the
star.

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:20.680
If we can see the light of the star shining
through around the planet, we can maybe

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:24.070
deduce some information about does it have
an atmosphere?

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:25.840
What are the properties of that atmosphere?

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:28.120
How hot is it? What's it made out of?

00:09:28.810 --> 00:09:33.940
That's how we'll be able to determine if
things in the atmosphere might indicate that

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:36.100
the surface is hospitable to life.

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:43.830
So far, we haven't seen any exoplanets with
atmospheres that we could live in.

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:46.890
But that's about to change.

